Propellerhead Remote Mapping File
File Format Version	1.0.0
Control Surface Manufacturer	Nuts
Control Surface Model	Nocturn
Map Version	2.0.0


//======================================================================
//  Novation Nocturn - Reason Remote map
//
//  Reason loads every scope for a control surface from a single .remotemap.
//  The Nocturn has no keybed and no pads, so there is no Master Keyboard
//  scope and no drum/pad mapping.
//
//  WHY ONLY "Reason Main Mixer Channel"
//  ------------------------------------
//  The surface is a channel strip, and there is exactly one scope that can
//  carry a four-page channel strip: "Reason Main Mixer Channel". It exposes
//  every channel target we need in ONE flat namespace ("Width", "C
//  Threshold", "FX1 Send Level", ...), so a page can pull from anywhere.
//
//  "Reason Master Section" was considered and rejected. Its channel-strip
//  targets are partitioned by a "Define Group Target TheGroup" whose values
//  are section names (Pan, EQ1, EQ2, Dyn1, Dyn2, Sends1, Sends2, Ins1,
//  Ins2), and a Map line carries only ONE group value. Our pages draw from
//  several sections at once - the Mixer page needs Pan + EQ1 + EQ2 - so no
//  single group value can serve a page. TheGroup also gates "Shift=Channel",
//  which Reason owns. The factory AlphaTrack map shows the consequence: it
//  drives each section from a separate physical button, one section at a
//  time. That is precisely the "Reason selects the variation" limitation we
//  are avoiding. The Master Section scope is for the master bus and FX
//  returns in any case, not for a channel strip.
//
//  THE SURFACE PAGES
//  -----------------
//  The 8 dials and the 8 green page-row buttons BANK. A page is a set of
//  parameters on those same physical controls, and the four pages are the
//  four sections of the selected mixer channel:
//
//    page 1  MIXER     the channel's input and filter strip
//    page 2  DYNAMICS  the channel's compressor and gate
//    page 3  EQ        the channel's 4-band EQ
//    page 4  FX        the channel's 4 insert rotaries + 4 sends
//
//  Paging is owned by the DRIVER, not by Reason. The driver sends a
//  different CC for a dial or button depending on the active page, steps
//  the page from the PermaBar, and never forwards the page buttons. Reason
//  therefore sees four separate control-surface item sets and never learns
//  that a page changed.
//
//  That is why each page's controls are separate ITEMS here - "Knob 1
//  Mixer" and "Knob 1 EQ" - rather than one item with a variation. A
//  .remotemap maps one item to exactly one remotable per scope, and Reason
//  evaluates the map statically: there is no way to re-point one item from
//  outside Reason. Duplicating the item set per page is the same technique
//  the Akai MPK Mini Play codec uses for its banks ("Knob 1 A" / "Knob 1 B").
//
//  "Define Group" variations would also give four mappings, but Reason owns
//  which variation is active, so Page -/+ stepping could not exist. The
//  driver-side CC tables (Midi:Paging in appsettings.json) are what let the
//  PermaBar step pages, so item-per-page is the approach that keeps the
//  whole design.
//
//  SHARED ACROSS EVERY PAGE: the Speed Dial and its click, and the
//  Crossfader. They keep one item and one mapping. The PermaBar page
//  buttons (Page -/+, and the MIXER / USER / INSTR / FX jumps) are not
//  items at all - the driver consumes them.
//
//  Page assignments (PermaBar silkscreen -> page):
//    MIXER -> Mixer      USER -> Dynamics
//    INSTR -> EQ         FX   -> FX
//    Page - / Page +     step back / forward, wrapping
//
//  UNMAPPED ITEMS. Pages 1 and 3 place their parameters on the odd dials
//  only (E1/E3/E5/E7), so Knob 2/4/6/8 of those pages are deliberately
//  unmapped - the gap separates function groups on the surface. Pages 2
//  and 4 use all 8 dials, because each has 8 continuous parameters and no
//  spare dial to leave a gap with. Button 8 is spare on pages 1 and 2. An
//  item with no Map line simply does nothing.
//
//  CONTINUOUS vs ON/OFF. Dials carry continuous parameters and the page-row
//  switches carry on/off states, because a page-row item is a Toggle: an
//  on/off value is the only thing it can set. The Dynamics page's gate
//  "Expander" is a switch on B4 for that reason, and the compressor's
//  Release / Fast Attack and the gate's Range / Release / Hold are dials,
//  not switches.
//
//  NAMES ARE VERIFIED. Every Remotable Item below was checked against the
//  targets in Reason's own factory .remotemap files for this scope. The
//  scope's names are not always the display names: the compressor is
//  "C Threshold" (not "Comp Threshold"), the gate's "Expander" has no
//  "Gate" prefix, and the sends are "FX1 Pre Fader" (not "FX1 Send Pre
//  Fader"). A misspelled target fails silently.
//======================================================================

Scope	Propellerheads	Reason Main Mixer Channel
//	Control Surface Item	Key	Remotable Item	Scale	Mode

Map	_Scope		"Reason Main Mixer Channel"

// --- Page 1: Mixer ----------------------------------------------------
Map	Knob 1 Mixer		Width
Map	Knob 3 Mixer		Input Gain
Map	Knob 5 Mixer		LPF Frequency
Map	Knob 7 Mixer		HPF Frequency

Map	Button 1 Mixer		Mute
Map	Button 2 Mixer		Invert Phase
Map	Button 3 Mixer		Insert Pre
Map	Button 4 Mixer		Dyn Post EQ
Map	Button 5 Mixer		LPF On
Map	Button 6 Mixer		HPF On
Map	Button 7 Mixer		Filters Dyn S/C

// --- Page 2: Dynamics -------------------------------------------------
// All 8 dials are used here, because the page has 8 continuous parameters:
// the compressor's Threshold/Ratio/Release/Fast Attack on E1-E4 (the top
// row) and the gate's Threshold/Range/Release/Hold on E5-E8 (the bottom
// row). The odd/even gap of pages 1 and 3 is NOT kept on this page: there
// simply is no spare dial, and leaving G Release/G Hold off the page was
// the bug this layout fixes.
//
// The switches carry only ON/OFF states. The compressor's Release and Fast
// Attack and the gate's Range / Release / Hold are values, so they are dials
// here rather than switches; that is what freed Button 6 for Key On and made
// room for the previously-unmapped C Release / G Release / G Hold.
Map	Knob 1 Dynamics		C Threshold
Map	Knob 2 Dynamics		C Ratio
Map	Knob 3 Dynamics		C Release
Map	Knob 4 Dynamics		C Fast Atk
Map	Knob 5 Dynamics		G Threshold
Map	Knob 6 Dynamics		G Range
Map	Knob 7 Dynamics		G Release
Map	Knob 8 Dynamics		G Hold

Map	Button 1 Dynamics		Comp On
Map	Button 2 Dynamics		C Peak
Map	Button 3 Dynamics		Gate On
Map	Button 4 Dynamics		Expander
Map	Button 5 Dynamics		G Fast Atk
Map	Button 6 Dynamics		Key On

// --- Page 3: EQ -------------------------------------------------------
Map	Knob 1 EQ		HF Gain
Map	Knob 3 EQ		HF Frequency
Map	Knob 5 EQ		HMF Gain
Map	Knob 7 EQ		HMF Frequency

Map	Button 1 EQ		EQ On
Map	Button 2 EQ		EQ E Mode
Map	Button 3 EQ		HF Bell
Map	Button 4 EQ		HF On
Map	Button 5 EQ		HMF On
Map	Button 6 EQ		LMF On
Map	Button 7 EQ		LF Bell
Map	Button 8 EQ		LF On

// --- Page 4: FX -------------------------------------------------------
Map	Knob 1 FX		Rotary 1
Map	Knob 2 FX		Rotary 2
Map	Knob 3 FX		Rotary 3
Map	Knob 4 FX		Rotary 4
Map	Knob 5 FX		FX1 Send Level
Map	Knob 6 FX		FX2 Send Level
Map	Knob 7 FX		FX3 Send Level
Map	Knob 8 FX		FX4 Send Level

Map	Button 1 FX		Button 1
Map	Button 2 FX		Button 2
Map	Button 3 FX		Button 3
Map	Button 4 FX		Button 4
Map	Button 5 FX		FX1 Pre Fader
Map	Button 6 FX		FX2 Pre Fader
Map	Button 7 FX		FX3 Pre Fader
Map	Button 8 FX		FX4 Pre Fader

// --- Shared: same on every page ---------------------------------------
Map	Speed Dial		Level
Map	Speed Dial Button		Solo
Map	Crossfader		Pan
